_:genid26 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <https://w3id.org/plato#CertaintyLevel> .
_:genid26 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#List> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Authority> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Abstract superclass for all reference entities that provide provenance\n        or semantic grounding for attestations. Subtypes include Source,\n        Dataset, Period, RelationType, and CertaintyLevel.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Authority> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Authority"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/plato#Candidate
<https://w3id.org/plato#Candidate> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Candidate> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        An algorithm-generated match candidate between two SpatialEntities, produced\n        by the PLACE clustering engine. Candidates carry a similarity score,\n        algorithm version, and review status. They are explicitly not\n        IdentityRelations — they become IdentityRelations only when confirmed\n        by a human reviewer (linked back via plato:promoted_from).\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Candidate> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Candidate"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/plato#CertaintyLevel
<https://w3id.org/plato#CertaintyLevel> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#CertaintyLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/plato#Authority> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#CertaintyLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A defined level of certainty for use in attestations, allowing\n        projects to standardise their confidence vocabulary.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#CertaintyLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Certainty Level"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/plato#Citation
<https://w3id.org/plato#Citation> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Citation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        One attestation's use of one Authority: the qualified form of\n        plato:sourced_by. Carries what is true of this citation rather than\n        of the source or of the attestation: the locator within the work\n        (page, folio, column, table cell, entry), and whether the attribution\n        was stated in the source or inferred editorially. It carries no date.\n        The date of a witness belongs on the Source (plato:source_timespan)\n        and the date of the claim on the Attestation's Timespan.\n\n        An attestation resting on several sources has several Citations,\n        each with its own locator. Where there is nothing to say about the\n        citation, plato:sourced_by alone is sufficient.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Citation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Citation"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor
<https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Agent> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/Agent> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A person, team, or automated process that creates attestations.\n        Aligned with both foaf:Agent and prov:Agent for provenance\n        chain-of-custody tracking.\n\n        Contributors should be identified using persistent identifiers where\n        possible. For persons, ORCID (https://orcid.org/) is strongly\n        recommended and may be required by specific implementations. ORCID\n        identifiers should use the full URI format (https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000)\n        rather than just the numeric portion, as ORCID is particular about\n        proper URI formatting for linked data interoperability.\n\n        For organizations, ROR (https://ror.org/) or ISNI identifiers are\n        recommended. Proper identification ensures accurate attribution,\n        enables citation, and supports research assessment.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Contributor"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/plato#Dataset
<https://w3id.org/plato#Dataset> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Dataset> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/plato#Authority> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Dataset> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A collection-level authority: a published gazetteer, a contributed\n        dataset, or an external authority corpus (GeoNames, Wikidata, TGN,\n        OSM, Pleiades). Carries metadata such as title, version, publisher,\n        licence, and URI.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Dataset> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Dataset"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/plato#Gazetteer
<https://w3id.org/plato#Gazetteer> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Gazetteer> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#Dataset> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Gazetteer> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A mutable, versioned workspace of SpatialEntities and their associated\n        Attestations, owned by a contributor or team. Replaces the WHG v3\n        concepts of Dataset, Dataset Collection, and Place Collection.\n        A Gazetteer can be incrementally edited, enriched, and corrected\n        without re-ingestion.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Gazetteer> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Gazetteer"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/plato#GazetteerGroup
<https://w3id.org/plato#GazetteerGroup> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#GazetteerGroup> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A thematic or organisational grouping of Gazetteers (e.g.\n        'EMEW — Gazetteer of Early Modern England and Wales').\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#GazetteerGroup> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Gazetteer Group"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry
<https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#Geometry> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A spatial representation of a SpatialEntity's extent or location. Geometries\n        are independent, reusable entities. The same Geometry may be referenced\n        by multiple Attestations across different SpatialEntities or time periods.\n\n        Note: GeometryCollection is not supported in some graph database\n        implementations. For heterogeneous geometry sets (e.g., both point and\n        polygon), use multiple Geometry entities linked through separate\n        attestations.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Geometry"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/plato#IdentityRelation
<https://w3id.org/plato#IdentityRelation> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#IdentityRelation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        An assertion that two SpatialEntities refer to the same real-world entity.\n        Carries its own provenance (who asserted it, on what basis),\n        certainty (how confident the assertion is), and identity semantics\n        (exactMatch, closeMatch, related).\n\n        Identity relations are distinct from scholarly entity-to-entity\n        relations, which describe substantive historical relationships\n        between different entities. They are also distinct from Candidates,\n        which are algorithm-generated candidates not yet confirmed by\n        human review.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#IdentityRelation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Identity Relation"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/plato#Name
<https://w3id.org/plato#Name> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Name> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A toponym or appellation for a SpatialEntity. Names are independent, reusable\n        entities. The association between a Name and a SpatialEntity is always mediated\n        by an Attestation, which provides temporal scope and provenance.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Name> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Name"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/plato#Organization
<https://w3id.org/plato#Organization> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Organization> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        An organization to which a Contributor is affiliated. This is a\n        lightweight class defined primarily to avoid ambiguity in ontology\n        visualizations. Organizations can be further described using properties\n        from FOAF, schema.org, or other ontologies.\n\n        Organizations should use persistent identifiers where possible:\n        - ROR (Research Organization Registry): https://ror.org/\n        - ISNI (International Standard Name Identifier)\n        - Wikidata QIDs\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Organization> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Organization"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/plato#Period
<https://w3id.org/plato#Period> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Period> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/plato#Authority> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Period> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A named historical period (e.g. 'Byzantine', 'Early Modern'),\n        optionally linked to PeriodO or defined locally. Includes temporal\n        bounds for period-based querying.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Period> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Period"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/plato#PropertyValue
<https://w3id.org/plato#PropertyValue> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#PropertyValue> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A quantitative or descriptive attribute of a SpatialEntity, expressed as a\n        property drawn from an external vocabulary together with its value.\n        Covers the many things a historical source says that are neither a\n        name, a location, a period nor a classification — populations,\n        valuations, elevations, recurrence rules for markets and fairs.\n\n        The property itself is identified by URI rather than enumerated here,\n        so the vocabulary stays open and delegated (Wikidata properties,\n        CIDOC-CRM, project vocabularies), in the same way that Type delegates\n        its classifications to AAT.\n\n        Values may be literals (with a datatype and optional unit) or\n        structured objects whose shape is declared by plato:value_type. As a\n        facet node, a PropertyValue may carry the qualification properties\n        (certainty, fuzziness) like any other.\n\n        Where the value is itself an entity, use a entity-to-entity relation\n        instead; this class is for attributes, not relationships.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#PropertyValue> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Property Value"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/plato#RelationType
<https://w3id.org/plato#RelationType> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#RelationType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/plato#Authority> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#RelationType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A vocabulary entry defining a semantic relationship between SpatialEntities:\n        'capital_of', 'successor_to', 'connected_by_trade_route_to', etc.\n        Carries a label, an inverse label, and domain/range constraints.\n        This allows the vocabulary of historical relationships to grow\n        without modifying the core ontology.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#RelationType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Relation Type"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/plato#Source
<https://w3id.org/plato#Source> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Source> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/plato#Authority> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Source> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A citable source of evidence: a manuscript, chronicle, tax record,\n        map, scholarly article, or other document. Sources are the primary\n        provenance anchor for attestations. A Source may reference its parent\n        Dataset via plato:partOf.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Source> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Source"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity
<https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Entity> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        An entity whose identity is bound up with space, whether its spatial\n        extent is attested directly, applies notionally (as with the region to\n        which a period applies), or is derived from its constituents (as with a\n        collection). A SpatialEntity need not have any attested geometry: lost,\n        imagined and unlocated entities are admissible.\n\n        What kind of entity it is (settlement, route, region, administrative\n        unit, period and so on) is not fixed by the class. It is established by\n        Type attestations, so a classification is a claim rather than an axiom.\n\n        SpatialEntities are not defined by any single attestation. They are the\n        point of convergence for many attestations from different sources,\n        periods and contributors.\n\n        A SpatialEntity is not a place, and PLATO defines no Place class.\n        \"Place\" carries several meanings in the gazetteer community, and\n        which of them, if any, corresponds to a SpatialEntity is contested;\n        the ontology does not settle it. Neither a SpatialEntity nor any set\n        of attestations or of SpatialEntities is a place by virtue of this\n        ontology. Where the word appears in this document it is used\n        informally, for whatever a source is talking about.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Spatial Entity"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan
<https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://www.w3.org/2006/time#ProperInterval> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A temporal interval with start and end bounds. Timespans are\n        independent, reusable entities that may be shared across multiple\n        Attestations. Precision and uncertainty are expressed through\n        the start and end bound properties. Aligned with W3C Time Ontology\n        and compatible with PeriodO period definitions.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Timespan"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/plato#Type
<https://w3id.org/plato#Type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Type> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Type> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A classification or category for a SpatialEntity, typically drawn from a\n        controlled vocabulary such as the Getty Art & Architecture Thesaurus\n        (AAT). Types are independent, reusable entities.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Type> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Type"@en .
